1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:03,287
On a freezing afternoon in
December nineteen forty

2
00:00:03,297 --> 00:00:03,900
two,

3
00:00:03,910 --> 00:00:07,300
a group of scientists stood on a
balcony inside an

4
00:00:07,310 --> 00:00:09,250
old squash court in Chicago.

5
00:00:10,083 --> 00:00:12,803
In front of them was a strange,
dark mound,

6
00:00:13,203 --> 00:00:16,653
built from about forty five
thousand blocks of graphite and

7
00:00:16,663 --> 00:00:17,463
tonnes of uranium.

8
00:00:18,417 --> 00:00:21,397
If their calculations were
wrong, it might run out of
control.

9
00:00:21,937 --> 00:00:25,187
If they were right, it would be
the first time humans had ever

10
00:00:25,197 --> 00:00:27,567
released the power locked inside
the atom,

11
00:00:27,577 --> 00:00:28,457
on purpose.

12
00:00:29,333 --> 00:00:32,243
The man in charge was an Italian
physicist named

13
00:00:32,253 --> 00:00:33,453
Enrico Fermi.

14
00:00:33,793 --> 00:00:35,813
This is how he harnessed nuclear
energy.

15
00:00:37,483 --> 00:00:40,753
Fermi was born in Rome in
nineteen oh one.

16
00:00:40,763 --> 00:00:44,253
As a boy, he taught himself
physics from old textbooks

17
00:00:44,263 --> 00:00:45,703
he bought at a market.

18
00:00:46,500 --> 00:00:48,840
When he applied to university at
seventeen,

19
00:00:49,200 --> 00:00:52,590
his entrance essay on the
physics of vibrating strings was

20
00:00:52,600 --> 00:00:55,670
so advanced that the examiner
said it was worthy of a

21
00:00:55,680 --> 00:00:56,480
doctorate.

22
00:00:56,750 --> 00:00:59,290
By twenty five, he was a
professor in Rome,

23
00:00:59,650 --> 00:01:03,020
leading a group of brilliant
young physicists nicknamed the

24
00:01:03,030 --> 00:01:06,380
Via Panisperna boys, after the
street where their

25
00:01:06,390 --> 00:01:07,190
lab stood.

26
00:01:07,750 --> 00:01:11,060
His students nicknamed him the
Pope, because on questions of

27
00:01:11,070 --> 00:01:11,870
physics,

28
00:01:11,990 --> 00:01:13,370
he never seemed to be wrong.

29
00:01:14,083 --> 00:01:17,313
In nineteen thirty three, he
wrote a theory of radioactive

30
00:01:17,323 --> 00:01:20,263
beta decay that introduced a new
force of nature,

31
00:01:20,803 --> 00:01:21,603
the weak force.

32
00:01:22,083 --> 00:01:25,273
The journal Nature rejected it,
saying it was too remote from

33
00:01:25,283 --> 00:01:26,263
physical reality.

34
00:01:27,900 --> 00:01:30,630
The atom has a tiny, dense
centre called the nucleus,

35
00:01:30,640 --> 00:01:32,700
made of protons and neutrons.

36
00:01:33,500 --> 00:01:36,710
In nineteen thirty two, the
English physicist James

37
00:01:36,720 --> 00:01:38,540
Chadwick had discovered the
neutron.

38
00:01:39,417 --> 00:01:42,427
In nineteen thirty four, Fermi's
team started firing

39
00:01:42,437 --> 00:01:44,607
neutrons at every element they
could find,

40
00:01:44,617 --> 00:01:45,997
to see what would happen.

41
00:01:46,667 --> 00:01:50,017
Neutrons have no electric
charge, so they can slip right

42
00:01:50,027 --> 00:01:52,367
into a nucleus, and make it
unstable.

43
00:01:53,250 --> 00:01:54,850
Then they noticed something
strange.

44
00:01:55,510 --> 00:01:58,550
The results changed depending on
what the table was made of.

45
00:01:59,167 --> 00:02:02,537
Experiments on a wooden table
gave far stronger effects than

46
00:02:02,547 --> 00:02:03,367
on a marble one.

47
00:02:04,083 --> 00:02:05,403
Fermi had a hunch.

48
00:02:05,763 --> 00:02:09,013
He put a block of paraffin wax
between the neutrons

49
00:02:09,023 --> 00:02:11,933
and the target, and the
radioactivity shot up,

50
00:02:11,943 --> 00:02:13,483
a hundred times stronger.

51
00:02:14,333 --> 00:02:17,083
The hydrogen in the wax, and in
the wooden table,

52
00:02:17,093 --> 00:02:18,913
slowed the neutrons down.

53
00:02:19,293 --> 00:02:22,573
And slow neutrons were far
better at being captured by
nuclei.

54
00:02:23,333 --> 00:02:26,743
This discovery of slow neutrons
would turn out to be the key

55
00:02:26,753 --> 00:02:27,933
to the nuclear reactor.

56
00:02:29,733 --> 00:02:30,753
But Italy was changing.

57
00:02:31,453 --> 00:02:34,403
Mussolini's government passed
racial laws in nineteen thirty

58
00:02:34,413 --> 00:02:34,683
eight,

59
00:02:34,693 --> 00:02:36,633
aimed at Jewish people.

60
00:02:37,353 --> 00:02:39,533
Fermi's wife, Laura, was Jewish.

61
00:02:40,500 --> 00:02:43,970
That November, Fermi learned he
had won the Nobel Prize in

62
00:02:43,980 --> 00:02:44,780
Physics.

63
00:02:45,167 --> 00:02:47,427
He used the trip to Stockholm as
an escape.

64
00:02:47,967 --> 00:02:51,267
After collecting the prize, the
family never went home.

65
00:02:51,667 --> 00:02:53,287
They sailed straight on to New
York.

66
00:02:54,900 --> 00:02:57,460
Just weeks later, news arrived
from Germany.

67
00:02:57,920 --> 00:03:00,800
Otto Hahn and Fritz Strassmann
had found that uranium,

68
00:03:01,420 --> 00:03:03,480
hit by neutrons, could split in
two.

69
00:03:04,167 --> 00:03:06,557
Lise Meitner and Otto Frisch
explained it,

70
00:03:06,567 --> 00:03:07,667
and named it fission.

71
00:03:08,500 --> 00:03:11,910
Fermi's team had probably split
uranium atoms back in

72
00:03:11,920 --> 00:03:12,190
Rome,

73
00:03:12,200 --> 00:03:14,960
four years earlier, without
realising it.

74
00:03:15,583 --> 00:03:18,913
When a uranium nucleus splits,
it releases a burst

75
00:03:18,923 --> 00:03:22,103
of energy, and two or three new
neutrons.

76
00:03:22,917 --> 00:03:25,057
Fermi and others saw what that
meant.

77
00:03:25,557 --> 00:03:28,767
If those neutrons split more
atoms, which freed more

78
00:03:28,777 --> 00:03:29,577
neutrons,

79
00:03:29,717 --> 00:03:30,857
you could get a chain reaction.

80
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:33,960
Controlled, it could power
cities.

81
00:03:34,700 --> 00:03:36,620
Uncontrolled, it could become a
bomb.

82
00:03:37,417 --> 00:03:40,827
The Hungarian physicist Leo
Szilard had dreamed up the idea
of

83
00:03:40,837 --> 00:03:42,317
a chain reaction years before.

84
00:03:42,857 --> 00:03:45,887
In nineteen thirty nine, he
persuaded Albert Einstein

85
00:03:45,897 --> 00:03:48,437
to sign a letter warning
President Roosevelt.

86
00:03:50,400 --> 00:03:52,080
The Second World War had begun.

87
00:03:52,680 --> 00:03:55,950
Scientists feared that Nazi
Germany might build an atomic

88
00:03:55,960 --> 00:03:56,840
bomb first.

89
00:03:57,667 --> 00:03:59,987
The United States launched a
secret project,

90
00:04:00,407 --> 00:04:03,717
and Fermi was asked to prove
that a chain reaction was

91
00:04:03,727 --> 00:04:04,527
even possible.

92
00:04:05,250 --> 00:04:08,540
His team built a reactor in a
squash court under the

93
00:04:08,550 --> 00:04:11,420
stands of Stagg Field, the
University of

94
00:04:11,430 --> 00:04:13,530
Chicago's abandoned football
stadium.

95
00:04:14,333 --> 00:04:17,433
They called it a pile, because
that's exactly what it was:

96
00:04:17,933 --> 00:04:21,123
a pile of graphite bricks, layer
after layer,

97
00:04:21,133 --> 00:04:23,213
with lumps of uranium tucked
inside.

98
00:04:24,083 --> 00:04:27,443
The graphite did the same job as
Fermi's paraffin wax.

99
00:04:28,243 --> 00:04:31,483
It slowed the neutrons down, so
they would split more uranium.

100
00:04:32,417 --> 00:04:35,387
Running through the pile were
control rods coated with

101
00:04:35,397 --> 00:04:36,197
cadmium,

102
00:04:36,437 --> 00:04:38,137
a metal that soaks up neutrons.

103
00:04:38,797 --> 00:04:40,757
Pull them out, and the reaction
grows.

104
00:04:41,557 --> 00:04:43,017
Push them in, and it dies.

105
00:04:43,833 --> 00:04:47,013
There was no radiation shield,
and no cooling system.

106
00:04:47,533 --> 00:04:50,923
Their safety plan included a rod
on a rope that one man could

107
00:04:50,933 --> 00:04:54,283
cut with an axe, and three young
physicists standing

108
00:04:54,293 --> 00:04:56,893
on top with buckets of cadmium
solution.

109
00:04:57,833 --> 00:04:59,633
They were nicknamed the suicide
squad.

110
00:05:01,400 --> 00:05:03,290
On the morning of the second of
December,

111
00:05:03,300 --> 00:05:06,670
nineteen forty two, Fermi
ordered the control

112
00:05:06,680 --> 00:05:09,200
rods to be pulled out, step by
step.

113
00:05:09,833 --> 00:05:12,643
After each step, he checked the
neutron counters,

114
00:05:12,653 --> 00:05:16,063
and calculated with his pocket
slide rule exactly

115
00:05:16,073 --> 00:05:17,353
what should happen next.

116
00:05:18,167 --> 00:05:20,227
The counters clicked faster and
faster.

117
00:05:20,907 --> 00:05:23,207
Then, at about half past three
in the afternoon,

118
00:05:23,407 --> 00:05:25,447
Fermi closed his slide rule and
smiled.

119
00:05:26,333 --> 00:05:28,773
The reaction is self-sustaining,
he announced.

120
00:05:29,413 --> 00:05:32,483
For the first time, a nuclear
chain reaction was running

121
00:05:32,493 --> 00:05:33,713
under human control.

122
00:05:34,583 --> 00:05:36,883
It ran for about four and a half
minutes,

123
00:05:37,143 --> 00:05:40,203
producing about half a watt,
less than a small torch bulb.

124
00:05:40,903 --> 00:05:42,683
Then Fermi ordered the rods back
in.

125
00:05:43,417 --> 00:05:46,787
The pile was later taken apart
and rebuilt outside the

126
00:05:46,797 --> 00:05:47,507
city,

127
00:05:47,517 --> 00:05:50,337
in the forest that became
Argonne National Laboratory.

128
00:05:51,333 --> 00:05:54,693
Someone produced a bottle of
Italian Chianti wine.

129
00:05:55,293 --> 00:05:58,163
They drank from paper cups, and
signed the bottle's straw

130
00:05:58,173 --> 00:05:58,973
wrapper.

131
00:05:59,417 --> 00:06:01,457
The project's leader, Arthur
Compton,

132
00:06:02,057 --> 00:06:03,437
phoned Washington in code.

133
00:06:03,977 --> 00:06:06,837
The Italian navigator has landed
in the new world,

134
00:06:07,157 --> 00:06:07,927
he said.

135
00:06:07,937 --> 00:06:08,737
How were the natives?

136
00:06:09,297 --> 00:06:10,097
Very friendly.

137
00:06:11,567 --> 00:06:14,997
Fermi's reactor opened the door
to the Manhattan Project,

138
00:06:15,007 --> 00:06:18,137
and in nineteen forty five, to
the atomic

139
00:06:18,147 --> 00:06:21,107
bombs that destroyed Hiroshima
and Nagasaki.

140
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:24,540
Fermi worked on the bomb at Los
Alamos.

141
00:06:25,060 --> 00:06:26,340
Like many of the scientists,

142
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:30,000
he lived with the consequences
for the rest of his life.

143
00:06:30,500 --> 00:06:33,530
After the war, he opposed
building the even more powerful

144
00:06:33,540 --> 00:06:34,420
hydrogen bomb.

145
00:06:35,083 --> 00:06:36,483
At the first test explosion,

146
00:06:37,103 --> 00:06:40,593
in the New Mexico desert, Fermi
dropped scraps of paper

147
00:06:40,603 --> 00:06:43,903
as the blast wave passed, and
from how far they flew,

148
00:06:44,343 --> 00:06:45,523
estimated its power.

149
00:06:45,923 --> 00:06:46,923
He was not far off.

150
00:06:48,567 --> 00:06:51,467
But the same principle could
also be used to make
electricity.

151
00:06:52,417 --> 00:06:55,567
In a nuclear power station, the
heat from fission boils

152
00:06:55,577 --> 00:06:56,857
water into steam.

153
00:06:57,377 --> 00:07:00,777
The steam spins a turbine, and
the turbine spins a generator.

154
00:07:01,667 --> 00:07:04,457
In December nineteen fifty one,
an experimental

155
00:07:04,467 --> 00:07:07,847
reactor in Idaho, called E B R

156
00:07:07,857 --> 00:07:09,587
one, lit four light bulbs.

157
00:07:10,367 --> 00:07:13,487
It was the first electricity
ever made from nuclear energy.

158
00:07:14,250 --> 00:07:17,680
In nineteen fifty four, a small
plant at Obninsk in

159
00:07:17,690 --> 00:07:20,800
the Soviet Union became the
first to feed nuclear power

160
00:07:20,810 --> 00:07:22,510
into an electricity grid.

161
00:07:22,930 --> 00:07:25,170
Britain and the United States
soon followed.

162
00:07:25,917 --> 00:07:29,067
Today, around four hundred
nuclear reactors produce

163
00:07:29,077 --> 00:07:31,837
roughly a tenth of the world's
electricity,

164
00:07:32,257 --> 00:07:35,607
without burning any fuel or
releasing carbon dioxide

165
00:07:35,617 --> 00:07:36,597
as they run.

166
00:07:37,333 --> 00:07:40,763
Nuclear power has its problems:
the radioactive waste must

167
00:07:40,773 --> 00:07:42,993
be stored safely for thousands
of years,

168
00:07:43,413 --> 00:07:46,723
and accidents like Chernobyl and
Fukushima showed the price

169
00:07:46,733 --> 00:07:47,673
of getting it wrong.

170
00:07:48,417 --> 00:07:51,107
But it remains one of the
largest sources of low-carbon

171
00:07:51,117 --> 00:07:52,477
electricity on Earth.

172
00:07:54,067 --> 00:07:56,597
Fermi died of cancer in nineteen
fifty four,

173
00:07:56,607 --> 00:07:58,707
at just fifty three years old.

174
00:07:59,417 --> 00:08:02,447
Element number one hundred,
fermium, is named

175
00:08:02,457 --> 00:08:03,357
after him.

176
00:08:03,937 --> 00:08:07,407
So is Fermilab, one of America's
great particle physics

177
00:08:07,417 --> 00:08:08,087
laboratories,

178
00:08:08,097 --> 00:08:11,007
and a whole family of particles,
the

179
00:08:11,017 --> 00:08:11,817
fermions.

180
00:08:12,500 --> 00:08:14,970
He was famous for estimating
anything, quickly,

181
00:08:14,980 --> 00:08:16,840
from a few simple facts.

182
00:08:17,500 --> 00:08:19,940
Physicists still call these
Fermi problems.

183
00:08:20,833 --> 00:08:23,663
And over lunch one day in
nineteen fifty,

184
00:08:23,673 --> 00:08:24,713
talking about aliens,

185
00:08:25,353 --> 00:08:28,153
he asked a question that
scientists still argue about:

186
00:08:28,593 --> 00:08:29,533
so where is everybody?

187
00:08:31,317 --> 00:08:33,737
From a squash court under a
football stadium,

188
00:08:34,137 --> 00:08:37,627
Enrico Fermi showed that the
energy inside the atom

189
00:08:37,637 --> 00:08:39,317
could be released, and
controlled.

190
00:08:40,167 --> 00:08:43,497
For better and for worse, we
have been living in his nuclear
age

191
00:08:43,507 --> 00:08:44,307
ever since.
